Chapter: AgentProtocol
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The AgentProtocol defines agent identity (Ed25519 public key),
 capability grants with direction and scope, and the message
 types exchanged over the trust network. Seven cover the
 capability and control vocabulary; two more — Annotate and
 Verdict — carry knowledge about code as opaque payload bytes,
 letting Works own the encoding while keeping kind dispatch at
 the protocol layer.

 We say:

Section: Identity and Addressing

  AgentIdentity = record {
    public-key : List Integer
  }

  Capability = record {
    name : Text,
    direction : Integer between 0 and 255,
    scope : Text
  }

  Scope = record {
    direction : Integer between 0 and 255,
    path : Text,
    duration : Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  }

Section: Denial and Interrupt Reasons

  DenialReason =
    | InsufficientTrust
    | PolicyViolation
    | Expired
    | CustomDenial (Text)

  InterruptReason =
    | Emergency
    | PolicyRevoked
    | ContextChanged
    | Timeout

  InterruptAction =
    | Pause
    | Abort
    | FallbackTo (List Integer)

  ExplainDepth =
    | Summary
    | Full
    | Exhaustive

Section: Reasoning Chain

  ChainLink =
    | Percept (Text) (Integer)
    | Belief (List (List Integer)) (Integer)
    | PolicyLink (List Integer) (Boolean) (Text)
    | Action (Text) (Text)
    | Anomaly (Text) (Text) (Text)

Section: Agent Message Types

 The first seven variants are the capability/control vocabulary —
 propose/grant/deny authorization, explain/narrate reasoning,
 interrupt/handoff control. They are about what an agent will or
 won't do.

 The last two — Annotate and Verdict — carry knowledge about code:
 a SignedAnnotation published by an author, and a Verdict signed
 by a reviewer responding to one. They live at the protocol layer
 alongside the capability messages so the trust filter can dispatch
 on kind, but their payloads cross the layer boundary as opaque
 bytes — the Works layer (RepoProtocol) owns the encoding of
 ProposalPayload and Verdict.

  AgentMessage =
    | MsgPropose (ProposeMsg)
    | MsgGrant (GrantMsg)
    | MsgDeny (DenyMsg)
    | MsgExplain (ExplainMsg)
    | MsgNarrate (NarrateMsg)
    | MsgInterrupt (InterruptMsg)
    | MsgHandoff (HandoffMsg)
    | MsgAnnotate (AnnotateMsg)
    | MsgVerdict (VerdictMsg)
    | MsgSyncOffer (SyncOfferMsg)
    | MsgSyncReply (SyncReplyMsg)
    | MsgWorkRequest (WorkRequestMsg)
    | MsgWorkReply (WorkReplyMsg)
    | MsgLocateRequest (LocateRequestMsg)
    | MsgLocateReply (LocateReplyMsg)
    | MsgAnnounceRequest (AnnounceRequestMsg)
    | MsgAnnounceReply (AnnounceReplyMsg)

  ProposeMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    to : AgentIdentity,
    capability : Capability,
    context : List (List Integer),
    expires : Integer,
    signature : List Integer
  }

  GrantMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    proposal : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    capability : Capability,
    scope : Scope,
    expires : Integer,
    conditions : List (List Integer),
    signature : List Integer
  }

  DenyMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    proposal : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    reason : DenialReason,
    evidence : List (List Integer),
    signature : List Integer
  }

  ExplainMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    to : AgentIdentity,
    subject : List Integer,
    depth : ExplainDepth,
    signature : List Integer
  }

  NarrateMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    in-reply-to : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    chain : List ChainLink,
    summary : Text,
    signature : List Integer
  }

  InterruptMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    to : AgentIdentity,
    reason : InterruptReason,
    action : InterruptAction,
    authority : List (List Integer),
    signature : List Integer
  }

  HandoffMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    to : AgentIdentity,
    capability : Capability,
    scope : Scope,
    expires : Integer,
    revoke-from : Boolean,
    audit-required : Boolean,
    signature : List Integer
  }

  AnnotateMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    payload : List Integer,
    signature : List Integer
  }

  VerdictMsg = record {
    id : List Integer,
    from : AgentIdentity,
    in-reply-to : List Integer,
    payload : List Integer,
    signature : List Integer
  }

 The last two — SyncOffer and SyncReply — carry a fact-store reconciliation
 over the same wire. An offer names the hashes a peer holds; a reply carries
 the facts the offering peer was missing. Both bodies are opaque payload
 bytes whose encoding belongs to the FactSync layer, exactly as Annotate and
 Verdict defer to Works. Content addressing makes the exchange conflict-free:
 a hash is present or it is not, and merging in either direction converges.

  SyncOfferMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    payload : List Integer
  }

  SyncReplyMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    payload : List Integer
  }

 Ask for one work, by the hash a quotation names. Get that work or nothing.

 The payload is opaque here for the same reason the sync payloads are: the Works
 layer owns the encoding of a work, and this layer only needs to dispatch on
 kind. What crosses is the wire form the compiler's own reader already decodes.

 An empty payload is the honest answer for "I do not have it". It is not an
 error: a peer that does not hold a work is the normal case, and the asker's job
 is to try the next peer. Neither message is signed, and that is deliberate --
 the reply is answerable by its own content. The asker hashes what comes back and
 checks it against the hash it asked for, so a lying peer is caught by the
 addressing rather than by trusting the peer. That is the same reason the gate
 can let the transport be untrusted.

 The payload is Text, where the sync payloads are bytes, and that is not
 inconsistency. A work's wire form IS text -- it is what `sdw-encode` produces
 and what the compiler's own reader decodes -- and content addressing here is
 over CCE bytes by decision, so handing the text across and letting the frame
 encode it keeps one representation rather than three. An empty payload is "not
 here".

  WorkRequestMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    hash : Text
  }

  WorkReplyMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    hash : Text,
    payload : Text
  }

 Ask who holds a work, rather than asking for the work. A quotation names a
 digest and nothing else, so an asker that has not already been introduced to a
 holder has no way to turn that digest into an address. This is the verb that
 turns it into one.

 The reply carries addresses and no content. That is the whole reason it is a
 separate verb from WorkRequest: an address cannot be checked, so it is never
 believed -- it is only used, by fetching from it and hashing what comes back.
 The registry therefore does not need to hold, or ever have held, the work it
 points at, and cannot be made responsible for the honesty of a peer it names.

 `peers` is a space-separated list of `host:port`, empty when the registry
 knows nobody who holds the digest. Empty is the ordinary answer and not an
 error, exactly as an empty work payload is: a registry that has not been told
 about a holder simply has not been told.

 Neither message is signed, for the same reason the work pair is not. A signed
 rumour is still a rumour, and the check that actually protects the asker
 happens after the fetch, against the content.

  LocateRequestMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    hash : Text
  }

  LocateReplyMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    hash : Text,
    peers : Text
  }

 A peer announcing what it holds. `address` is where the peer can be reached
 (`host:port`), and `hashes` is a space-separated list of the digests it is
 offering at that address. The reply is an acknowledgement carrying the count
 the registry recorded, so a peer can tell a registry that took its
 announcement from one that never heard it.

 The peer states its OWN address rather than the registry inferring it from the
 connection, because the address a registry would infer is the one the peer
 dialled from, and that is not the address anyone else reaches it on.

 The scaling ceiling is real and is not solved here: a peer holding the whole
 compiler announces thousands of digests in one message. When that bites, the
 answer is a compact summary (`Foreword chapter BloomFilter` /
 `ElasticBloom`) rather than a longer list, and a probabilistic summary is safe
 precisely because the fetch path already tolerates a miss on a named peer.

  AnnounceRequestMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    address : Text,
    hashes : Text
  }

  AnnounceReplyMsg = record {
    from : AgentIdentity,
    accepted : Integer
  }

Section: Message Accessors

  msg-id : AgentMessage -> List Integer
  msg-id (m) = when m
    is MsgPropose (p) -> p.id
    is MsgGrant (g) -> g.id
    is MsgDeny (d) -> d.id
    is MsgExplain (e) -> e.id
    is MsgNarrate (n) -> n.id
    is MsgInterrupt (i) -> i.id
    is MsgHandoff (h) -> h.id
    is MsgAnnotate (a) -> a.id
    is MsgVerdict (v) -> v.id
    is MsgSyncOffer (s) -> []
    is MsgSyncReply (s) -> []
    is MsgWorkRequest (w) -> []
    is MsgWorkReply (w) -> []
    is MsgLocateRequest (l) -> []
    is MsgLocateReply (l) -> []
    is MsgAnnounceRequest (a) -> []
    is MsgAnnounceReply (a) -> []

  msg-from : AgentMessage -> AgentIdentity
  msg-from (m) = when m
    is MsgPropose (p) -> p.from
    is MsgGrant (g) -> g.from
    is MsgDeny (d) -> d.from
    is MsgExplain (e) -> e.from
    is MsgNarrate (n) -> n.from
    is MsgInterrupt (i) -> i.from
    is MsgHandoff (h) -> h.from
    is MsgAnnotate (a) -> a.from
    is MsgVerdict (v) -> v.from
    is MsgSyncOffer (s) -> s.from
    is MsgSyncReply (s) -> s.from
    is MsgWorkRequest (w) -> w.from
    is MsgWorkReply (w) -> w.from
    is MsgLocateRequest (l) -> l.from
    is MsgLocateReply (l) -> l.from
    is MsgAnnounceRequest (a) -> a.from
    is MsgAnnounceReply (a) -> a.from

  msg-signature : AgentMessage -> List Integer
  msg-signature (m) = when m
    is MsgPropose (p) -> p.signature
    is MsgGrant (g) -> g.signature
    is MsgDeny (d) -> d.signature
    is MsgExplain (e) -> e.signature
    is MsgNarrate (n) -> n.signature
    is MsgInterrupt (i) -> i.signature
    is MsgHandoff (h) -> h.signature
    is MsgAnnotate (a) -> a.signature
    is MsgVerdict (v) -> v.signature
    is MsgSyncOffer (s) -> []
    is MsgSyncReply (s) -> []
    is MsgWorkRequest (w) -> []
    is MsgWorkReply (w) -> []
    is MsgLocateRequest (l) -> []
    is MsgLocateReply (l) -> []
    is MsgAnnounceRequest (a) -> []
    is MsgAnnounceReply (a) -> []

  msg-type-name : AgentMessage -> Text
  msg-type-name (m) = when m
    is MsgPropose (p) -> "Propose"
    is MsgGrant (g) -> "Grant"
    is MsgDeny (d) -> "Deny"
    is MsgExplain (e) -> "Explain"
    is MsgNarrate (n) -> "Narrate"
    is MsgInterrupt (i) -> "Interrupt"
    is MsgHandoff (h) -> "Handoff"
    is MsgAnnotate (a) -> "Annotate"
    is MsgVerdict (v) -> "Verdict"
    is MsgSyncOffer (s) -> "SyncOffer"
    is MsgSyncReply (s) -> "SyncReply"
    is MsgWorkRequest (w) -> "WorkRequest"
    is MsgWorkReply (w) -> "WorkReply"
    is MsgLocateRequest (l) -> "LocateRequest"
    is MsgLocateReply (l) -> "LocateReply"
    is MsgAnnounceRequest (a) -> "AnnounceRequest"
    is MsgAnnounceReply (a) -> "AnnounceReply"

